---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import DateFormatConverter from '../../components/DateFormatConverter.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Date Format Converter" description="Paste any date and see it instantly converted to ISO 8601, RFC 2822, Unix timestamp, and other common formats.">
  <DateFormatConverter client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why so many date formats exist</h2>
  <p>Different systems and standards emerged independently — ISO 8601 for unambiguous machine-readable dates, RFC 2822 for email headers, Unix timestamps for simple numeric comparison. Converting between them by hand invites off-by-one and timezone errors.</p>

  <h2>How to use this converter</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste a date in any common format.</li>
    <li>See it instantly converted to ISO 8601, RFC 2822, Unix timestamp, and other formats.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>"January 1, 2025" converts to ISO 8601 as <code>2025-01-01T00:00:00.000Z</code> and to a Unix timestamp as <code>1735689600</code>.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming a date string's timezone</strong> without checking — the same date string can represent different moments depending on assumed timezone.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing date formats within the same system</strong> (e.g. MM/DD/YYYY vs DD/MM/YYYY), which is ambiguous for dates where both day and month could be valid numbers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Converting a human-entered date into ISO 8601 for an API request</li>
    <li>Debugging a date parsing issue by seeing all common representations at once</li>
    <li>Preparing a date for an email header (RFC 2822) or a Unix-timestamp-based system</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this handle ambiguous formats like 01/02/2025?</strong> Ambiguous formats are interpreted using standard JavaScript date parsing conventions — when in doubt, use an unambiguous format like ISO 8601 as input.</p>
</ToolLayout>
